# AT A RESET OPTICAL FLOW NO LONGER CLEARS ITS FOURTEEN LUMINANCE IMAGES (2026-10-10, the own build's stage 3).
#
# WHAT AMD'S CODE DOES. A reset dispatch of optical flow begins with 18 clears: four of its scene-change images, and
# the two sets of seven luminance images (the current frame's and the previous frame's pyramid: 3840x2160 down to
# 60x34). 66 us of a reset dispatch's 230.
#
# WHO READS A LUMINANCE IMAGE, BY THE SOURCE (ffx_opticalflow.cpp at tag v2.3.0 and the seven pass files):
#   - the prepare-luma pass writes level 0 of THE CURRENT set, every pixel, in every dispatch (786 to 793), and the
#     pyramid pass writes its levels 1 to 6 from it (797 to 803): the current set's clear is overwritten whole before
#     anything reads it;
#   - the scene-change histogram reads the current set's level 0, after that;
#   - the search and the scale read both sets, AND RETURN BEFORE THEY LOAD ANYTHING while optical flow's frame index is
#     5 or less (ffx_opticalflow_callbacks_hlsl.h, IsSceneChanged: "if (FrameIndex() <= 5)"; the index is 0 at a reset
#     and one more for each dispatch). The previous set of a reset dispatch is the current set of the dispatch after it,
#     which writes it whole. By the sixth dispatch, the first that searches, both sets have been written whole five
#     times.
# So no load ever sees what the fourteen clears wrote. The four clears of the scene-change images stay: those are read.
#
# MEASURED: see docs\reports\fg-3\own-build-2026-10-06\stage-3 (the table of cuts). A reset dispatch only: a steady pair
# has no clear.
what at a reset optical flow clears its four scene-change images and no longer its fourteen luminance images, which are written whole before anything reads them
passes none
wave64 it edits ffx_opticalflow.cpp, which runs on every device, and no shader: every variant's containers are unchanged by it
file framegeneration/fsr3/internal/ffx_opticalflow.cpp
